=== Thread PRRT_kwDOQ6DXvM5yNfuB ===
Author: copilot-pull-request-reviewer
File: apps/web/src/hooks/useDropUpload.ts:70
DB ID: 2887184072
Body:
`existingByName` only indexes existing *file* children, so a drop-upload whose filename matches an existing *folder* name will be treated as a “new file”, uploaded to IPFS, then fail later in `addFilesToFolder()` due to name-collision (it checks all child names). Consider tracking all existing child names for collision detection, and only offering the Replace/Skip flow when the existing child is a file; for a folder-name collision, fail fast before uploading.
```suggestion
      const existingByName = new Map<string, string>(); // name → fileId
      const existingFolderNames = new Set<string>(); // names of existing folder children
      if (folder) {
        for (const child of folder.children) {
          if (child.type === 'file') {
            existingByName.set(child.name, child.id);
          } else if (child.type === 'folder') {
            existingFolderNames.add(child.name);
          }
        }
      }

      // Fail fast on file–folder name collisions (no Replace/Skip for folders)
      const folderNameConflicts = files.filter((f) => existingFolderNames.has(f.name));
      if (folderNameConflicts.length > 0) {
        useUploadStore
          .getState()
          .setError(
            `Cannot upload file(s) with the same name as an existing folder: ${folderNameConflicts
              .map((f) => f.name)
              .join(', ')}`
          );
        return false;
      }
```


=== Thread PRRT_kwDOQ6DXvM5yNfuW ===
Author: copilot-pull-request-reviewer
File: apps/web/src/services/bin.service.ts:240
DB ID: 2887184098
Body:
When `addToBin` successfully resolves/decrypts the file metadata (has `fileMeta.size` and `fileMeta.mimeType`), the `BinEntry` still keeps `size: 0` and `mimeType: ''`. The bin UI uses `entry.size`/`entry.mimeType` for display/sorting, so deleted files will still show as 0B with a generic icon even though the real values are available here. Consider setting `entry.size = fileMeta.size` and `entry.mimeType = fileMeta.mimeType` alongside `contentCid/contentSize`.


=== Thread PRRT_kwDOQ6DXvM5yNfuf ===
Author: copilot-pull-request-reviewer
File: apps/web/src/services/bin.service.ts:324
DB ID: 2887184110
Body:
Same as `addToBin`: in `addManyToBin`, after decrypting `fileMeta`, the `BinEntry` still leaves `size` as 0 and `mimeType` as empty. This keeps bin list size/icon/sorting incorrect even though `fileMeta.size`/`fileMeta.mimeType` are available. Consider populating `entry.size` and `entry.mimeType` when metadata resolution succeeds.


=== Thread PRRT_kwDOQ6DXvM5yNfuq ===
Author: copilot-pull-request-reviewer
File: apps/web/src/services/bin.service.ts:736
DB ID: 2887184125
Body:
In `unpinFileCids` legacy fallback, the comment says JSON.parse "will fail for encrypted file metadata". Encrypted file metadata is still valid JSON (`{iv,data}`), so JSON.parse will usually succeed; the real failure mode is that the parsed object won't have `cid/size`, so no content unpin/quota reclaim happens. Updating the comment would make the behavior clearer when debugging legacy entries.
```suggestion
    // Resolve IPNS and attempt to interpret file metadata as plaintext JSON.
    // NOTE: For encrypted file metadata ({ iv, data }), JSON.parse will usually
    // succeed, but the object will not have cid/size, so no content unpin or
    // quota reclaim happens (the original GAP-1 bug). Entries that hit this path
    // may log an error but will not crash the delete flow.
```

